<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document><name>ILLUSTRATIVE progress-ms-36 ascent (3DOF sim)</name><description>ILLUSTRATIVE ASCENT ECEF — Soyuz-2.1b Progress MS-36 (97P, Baikonur 31/6) — NOT OFFICIAL TELEMETRY
Method: first-principles 3DOF sim (/workspace/trajectory-deck/sim/ascent3dof.py): WGS-84 rotating Earth, J2, US76 drag,
TIMELINE mode: every ignition/cutoff/separation/fairing time is a published hard event; mdot=F_vac/(Isp_vac*g0); thrust SL/vac on ambient pressure.
FITTED (least squares vs published knots): vertical rise 9.38 s, kick 5.943 deg over 10 s, Cd 0.400, payload 7280 kg,
gravity turn, then linear-tangent steering tan(theta)=0.3988-0.001188*(t-t_ign stage 2).
Vehicle config: Soyuz-2.1b (RD-107A / RD-108A / RD-0124), no Fregat — TIMELINE mode (sim/vehicles/soyuz21b.json; every number carries a source URL, derivation, or ASSUMPTION).
Stage masses, thrust and Isp are the Soyuz-2 Wikipedia tables (2.1b third stage is RD-0124). Empty+propellant does not equal the table gross masses; the gaps are not added.
Event times are BORROWED from the Progress MS-19 Soyuz-2.1a sequence on RussianSpaceWeb, because no Soyuz-2.1b Progress timeline with times was found. Wikipedia&#x27;s generic burns are 118 / 286 / 270 s.
ILLUSTRATIVE. Progress MS-36 (97P), Baikonur Site 31/6, T0 2026-11-24T05:03:00Z. LL2 id 8dd5de11-831b-445f-86f0-bffefe0550d9, status To Be Confirmed, net precision Minute, window start=end 05:03Z, last_updated 2026-06-08T21:46:36Z. Next Spaceflight lists the same 05:03Z.
VEHICLE CONFLICT, not silent: this file flies Soyuz-2.1b (RD-0124). LL2 and Next Spaceflight name Soyuz-2.1b. Interfax, dateline 22 September with no year printed, quotes Roscosmos: Soyuz-2.1b was adapted for Progress MS, the first such launch was 16 September, and Soyuz-2.1a continues for satellites and crewed flights (https://www.interfax.ru/russia/1117752). Roscosmos TV http://www.tvroscosmos.ru/2833/ lists Progress MS-36 from Baikonur 31 on 24 Nov 2026 and does not name 2.1a or 2.1b. Wikipedia EN says Soyuz-2.1a, 09:03 UTC and Zvezda aft. Prior Progress flights cited here (MS-19, MS-23, MS-27, MS-28) flew Soyuz-2.1a.
Event times and the 193 x 240 km parking orbit are BORROWED from those Soyuz-2.1a flights. No MS-36 or Soyuz-2.1b Progress timeline was found. Docking target is Poisk (task / Next Spaceflight class of reports). Wikipedia says Zvezda. Docking is not modelled.
Stage-3 cutoff is ignition 286.159 s plus Wikipedia&#x27;s 270 s RD-0124 burn (556.159 s). The Soyuz-2.1a cutoff 525.45 s and the 8 min 49 s separation are recorded and are not the hard events: that shorter burn left this model suborbital. Separation is 4 s after the 2.1b cutoff (560.159 s), the same gap as MS-19&#x27;s 525.45 s to 529 s.
Target (see launch config): insertion 193 km, apoapsis 240 km, fpa 0, launch azimuth 63.39 deg; payload 7280 kg.
Pad: 45.996034N 63.564003E. Frame: WGS-84 ECEF, positions km, velocities km/s (Earth-relative).
T0 used: 2026-11-24T05:03:00Z. Re-run with another T0 by shifting utc_iso only; ECEF geometry is Earth-fixed.
POST-CUTOFF: after final cutoff T+556.2 s the stage coasts (same WGS-84/J2/US76-drag model) to PAYLOAD DEPLOY T+560 s; rows tagged -coast/-burn.
POST-CUTOFF deploy T+560 s (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soyuz-2 (270 s RD-0124 burn) and https://www.russianspaceweb.com/progress-ms-19.html (4 s from cutoff to separation, borrowed)): alt 191.86 km, osculating 179.3 x 218.0 km, i 49.083 deg.</description>
